  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <p>Lac du Revest, also called Barrage du Revest or Lac de Dardennes, is a 10-hectare lake below the village of Le Revest-les-Eaux, in the Var, about 8 km from central Toulon. The Provence Méditerranée tourist office states that dogs on a leash are accepted and that swimming is banned, for dogs as for people.</p>

      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The dam was built in 1912. The lake, with a capacity of one million m³, is fed by several springs, almost all submerged and always abundant, including the Ragas resurgence. Overlooked by hills covered in Aleppo pines and oaks, the emerald-green lake is described by the tourist office as a calm place, popular for walks and with anglers; it is a Natura 2000 site. The woods along the lake are a starting point for hikes in the surrounding hills.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Access is free and open every day all year. Your dog is welcome as long as it is kept on a leash, but it must not enter the water: swimming is banned, and the Dardennes reservoir stores water for Toulon's drinking-water supply (study by the Toulon Provence Méditerranée metropolitan authority). The Var also bans camping and fires outside designated and regulated areas.</p>
      <p>The dam is very close to the GR 99, which runs as far as the Verdon, and the GR 51 (Balcons de la Méditerranée); the “Tourrevelle loop” of the GR 51 leads to the dam and follows the reservoir along its whole western side, with open views of the dam and the lake, according to the metropolitan authority. Fishing is allowed; the most common species are roach and perch, along with pike and carp.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: walk around the lake on a leash and keep your dog out of the water; it is a cooler, calmer outing than the coastal beaches in summer.</div>

      <h2>Worth checking before you go</h2>
      <p>No precise rule on leashes, zones or seasons is published beyond the tourist office's mention: follow the signage on-site. The official sources read do not describe parking spaces.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The village of Le Revest-les-Eaux has a 13th-century medieval keep with a view over the lake, the Saint-Christophe church (1679) and a museum of sacred art, with a Provençal market on Saturday mornings at Place Meiffret, according to the metropolitan authority.</p>
    </div>

